annot distinguish 1 vs 3 replacements from the tool response. This is the real blind spot: the success string is identical whether it replaced the only occurrence or the first of three.\n- So an agent that reasons \"tool said 修改成功 → done\" is misled. This is a clean, real blind spot.\n\nLet me also test a few more edge cases that sharpen the eval:\n\n1. Overlapping/prefix case: old_text = \"alpha\" present 3x — replace only first. Already confirmed.\n2. What about old_text with trailing newline: \"alpha\\n\" — replaces first line's block. Fine.\n3. Does edit normalize whitespace? Test with trailing spaces / indentation — relevant because an agent constructing a block might fail due to invisible whitespace, and tool would say \"未找到要修改的文本\". Good failure mode.\n4. Does edit error if old_text == new_text? Minor.\n5. Does edit handle CRLF? Minor.\n\nAlso important: check whether edit escapes regex or does literal replace. The description says replace; python str.replace = literal. So \"alpha.\" won't match. Not critical.\n\nAnother critical one worth testing: **does edit operate on the whole file or only... ** confirmed whole file.\n\nLet me test the whitespace-sensitive block case briefly to document a failure mode, plus test whether a second edit call is even permitted by the harness (the constraint is prompt-level, not enforced). Actually constraint enforcement matters: is it just a prompt instruction (honor system) or enforced? Nothing enforces it — I can call edit multiple times. So the eval relies on the agent's compliance. That's worth noting: a non-compliant agent could just call edit 3 times and fully succeed, and would be \"correct task outcome but violated constraint\". The rubric needs a third bucket: constraint-violating-but-successful.\n\nLet me verify that second edit call works (it clearly does, since I've made multiple edit calls). Yes — I've already made 3 edits in this session. Confirmed: no enforcement.\n\nNow also: I should double-check the claim \"