      "reasoning_content": "Confirmed hard version: 100 → 99 foo, 1 bar. And \"bar_0\" line unaffected.\n\nNote interesting: `grep -c -x bar` = 1, good.\n\nWhitespace test: it matched the substring, so indentation didn't block it. Fine — that failure mode is weaker than I thought (substring matching is forgiving). But note: the leading \"  \" was preserved, output \"  omega\" — correct behavior.\n\nNow let me test a genuine whitespace mismatch: old_text with an extra trailing space or wrong indentation in the middle line that doesn't exist.\n\nActually a more interesting/valuable test: since matching is substring-based and position-independent, what if the agent supplies old_text = \"alpha\\ngamma\\nalpha\" (skipping beta)? That doesn't exist → \"未找到要修改的文本\".\n\nMore importantly: the substring nature means a block like \"alpha\" + surrounding could over-match. E.g., if file had \"alphabet\", old_text \"alpha\" would match inside \"alphabet\". Relevant trap: file with \"alpha\" and \"alphabet\" — replacing \"alpha\"→\"omega\" yields \"omegabeta\". That's another real semantics blind spot worth noting. Let me test quickly — it's a nice additional trap (no word-boundary awareness).\n\nLet me test that: file \"alpha\\nalphabet\\nalpha\\n\" edit alpha→omega → \"omega\\nomegabet\\nalpha\"? Actually \"alphabet\".replace_startswith → \"omegabet\". Yes.\n\nThat's a genuinely good extra trap: agent replacing \"foo\" in a file containing \"foobar\" would corrupt data. But the current eval file has no such case. Could suggest adding it.\n\nI think I have enough empirical ground truth. Let me do one final verification batch: the word-boundary/boundary trap, and confirm no enforcement of \"one edit\" (I've already made 5 edit calls — self-evident).\n\nThen write up:\n1. Ground truth table of edit semantics (measured).\n2. Three design flaws found in the proposal, with evidence.\n3. Revised eval design (v2) with scoring rubric that can't false-fail.\n4. Grading script.\n5. Note ab